            <ArticleTitle>Intragastric balloon left in the stomach for more than a year: two case reports.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>436-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000341134</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Intragastric balloon (IGB) is often used to achieve short-term weight reduction in morbidly obese patients. It is placed endoscopically for a 6-month-period either in the pre-bariatric surgery setting and also in cases where no bariatric surgery is planned. Prolonged implantation may be accompanied by serious complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We herein report two cases of extraction of intact IGBs long time after their placement.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although no adverse events were observed, adherence to manufacturers' recommendations is definitely suggested.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
